# SYNOPSIS

```usage
mkdir [-p] [-m mode] name ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`mkdir` makes a directory for each <name>, with the mode `0777` less the file mode creation mask.
It is sbase's command, in `/boot/bin/posix`: the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) binds that directory before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it there.

: `-m` <mode>
  Make each directory with <mode>, octal or symbolic as chmod(1) takes it, symbolic modes starting from `a=rwx`.
: `-p`
  Make any missing directories on the way to <name> too, each `0777` less the mask but always writable and searchable by its owner, and do not complain of a <name> that is already a directory.

# EXAMPLES

sbase's test (`tests/posix/sbasetest.c`) runs this in the POSIX namespace, and checks that it exits 0:

```text
mkdir -p /tmp/sb/sub
```

# SEE ALSO

rmdir(1), chmod(1), ls(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/mkdir.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A directory that cannot be made is reported, as `mkdir: mkdir /tmp/x: File exists`, and the next <name> is tried; with `-p`, a <name> that is something other than a directory is `mkdir: x: Not a directory`.
If any fails the exit status is 1.
A malformed <mode> is `mkdir: x: invalid mode`, and exits 1.

# BUGS

On VectraOS the file mode creation mask is always `022`: `umask` changes nothing.
The C library makes a directory with its mode's `0755` bits at most, so `-m` cannot give the group or others write permission, nor the set-id and sticky bits; chmod(1) can afterwards.
